    "conclusion": "The evidence supports a real Site A speed advantage of roughly 5%–9% after accounting for order size, product mix, shift, and staffing pressure. The scanner improves individual picking time by about 4% (95% CI: 1%–7%), so it is a contributing cause, but it is not the primary or sole explanation for the site-level gap: Site A remained about 5% faster during scanner outages. Reject the explanation that the observed gap is merely a timestamp artifact, because within-site measurements were reliable and the cross-site offset was reproducible; reject scanner availability alone as the complete mechanism. Retain differences associated with operational complexity—especially processes affecting high-complexity orders—and other persistent site-level workflow or organizational factors as plausible competing explanations requiring further study."
